<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Règlement Organique (Mount Lebanon)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1861</span> · Also known as Reglement Organique of 1861</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Règlement Organique was the statute agreed in 1861 between the Ottoman government and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> after the civil war of 1860 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>. It made the mountain a single autonomous district, the Mutasarrifate, governed by a non-Lebanese Ottoman Christian governor with a council representing its religious communities. Revised in 1864, it remained in force until 1915.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reglement-organique-mount-lebanon.jpg" alt="Règlement Organique (Mount Lebanon)" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Adolphe Beaucé · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_expeditionary_corps_landing_in_Beyrouth_16_August_1860.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
